## What to generate

### File 1: __PROJECT_DIR__/config_overrides.sh

A bash script sourced by the harness. Use `: "${VAR:=value}"` syntax.

Must set at minimum:
- `TEST_CASE_DIR` — root of the C source (from the user's description)
- `RUST_DIR` — where transpiled Rust goes (default: `__PROJECT_DIR__/rust`)
- `WORK_DIR` — runtime output directory (default: `__PROJECT_DIR__`)

Determine from reading the actual source tree:
- `C_SRC_DIRS` — directories containing .c files (default: `$TEST_CASE_DIR/src`)
- `C_INCLUDE_DIRS` — directories containing .h files (default: `$TEST_CASE_DIR/include`)
- `CONFIGS_FILE` — if multi-config, point to a configs.txt you also create
- `COVERAGE_MODES` — typically `function`
- `MODULES` — modular transpilation. Inspect the C source layout:
  - If the .c files are organized into named subdirectories under `C_SRC_DIRS`
    (e.g., `src/crypto/`, `src/math/`, `src/io/`) and each subdirectory represents
    a coherent, independently-compilable unit: set `MODULES="auto"` (the harness
    will detect subdirectories automatically) or list them explicitly as
    `MODULES="crypto,math,io"`.
  - If the .c files are a flat collection (all directly under `C_SRC_DIRS` with no
    meaningful subdirectory grouping): leave `MODULES=""` (default, single-unit).
  - A library is "highly modular" when: it has 3+ subdirectories each with 5+ .c
    files, each subdir has its own header group, and the subdirs have minimal
    cross-dependencies. When in doubt, prefer `MODULES=""` (single-unit is safe;
    modular just speeds up large projects by parallelising transpilation).

If multi-config, override `get_compile_args()`, `get_cargo_features()`,
`get_c_sources_for_config()`.

If files need excluding, override `get_c_sources()`.

If the user mentions an existing test runner, set `DIFFTEST_SCRIPT` and/or
